  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-2$ and $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+1.$ If $R=[-3,2]\\times [-2,3],$ and\n\n$$\n{\u222c}_{D}{2}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={\u222c}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\u2216D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R\u2216D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R\u2216D\\end{aligned}$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14099625/assets/414a5c8c14d70ff08d607c2a755f565c147b8b5e691a8d38a9e7db5697ba9e48.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal ${2}^{x+y}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\u222c}_{D}{2}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={\u222c}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function I. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,\u2216D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(0,3)\\in R\\,\u2216\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\n- Functions II and III equal ${2}^{x+y}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
